      "evidence_type": "published tolerance of a different insertion (transposon, FP fusion)",
      "extracellular": true,
      "functional_impact_measured": "Functional assessment showed minimal perturbation: normal internalization kinetics after WIN55,212 (10 \u00b5M) and intact calcium signaling responses to 2-AG (L12). Plasma-membrane-exclusive labeling confirmed, discriminating surface from intracellular receptor (L10/L11).",
      "gene_symbol": "CNR1",
      "insert_after_residue": 179,
      "library": null,
      "linker": null,
      "loop_run_position": null,
      "median_conservation": null,
      "numbered_against": null,
      "plddt": null,
      "position_evidence": "validated",
      "position_frame": null,
      "provenance": "literature_retrieved",
      "rationale": "L6 explicitly names phenylalanine at position 180 (F180) in CB1R as the site of TCO*A incorporation; Python confirms res180=F within ECL1 (topology landmarks 176\u2013187). The modification replaces F180, so insert_after_residue=179 (between V179 and F180) faithfully represents the junction; cited_tag_residue=180. The site is 5 residues from TM2 end (175) and 8 residues from TM3 start (188), both \u22653. Evidence type is 'tolerance of a different insertion' because TCO*A is a single unnatural amino acid substitution rather than a classical epitope-tag insertion, but L12 confirms full functional preservation. Full-length CB1R with TCO*A incorporated at position F180 (replacing phenylalanine 180) in ECL1, expressed in mammalian cells; plasma-membrane-exclusive labeling achieved with tetrazine-dye via bioorthogonal click chemistry. Construct also carries an SSF tag at the N-terminus. Screened against multiple positions; F180 was the most successful for receptor expression and efficient TCO*A incorporation.",
      "residue_after": "F",
      "residue_before": "V",
      "residue_label": "V179",
      "residue_range": null,
      "site_id": "CNR1-internal-179-lit",
      "site_kind": "internal",
      "source_tier": "paper",
      "sources": [
        {
          "citation": "DOI 10.1101/2024.03.21.586146",
          "claim": "We used a CB1-GFP construct to screen several positions for the most receptor expression and efficient TCO*A incorporation, and the most successful site was found to be by replacing the phenylalanine at position 180 in CB1R ( CB1-F180)(Figure 3B and 4A).",
          "source_id": "DOI:10.1101/2024.03.21.586146"
        }
      ],
